      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>Feedback from three people working in that field on what is still missing from your plan.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">The money filter: the college is not the whole budget</h2>

  <p>
    Your fee is one line in a bigger budget. As a strict planning heuristic, keep the college fee near 10% of
    everything your family can spend on your education.
  </p>

  <p>
    With Rs 10 lakh in total, that means about Rs 1 lakh for the college. The other Rs 9 lakh covers living
    costs, books, tools, projects, short courses and later upskilling. Spending above roughly 20% is a serious
    caution that needs course-specific proof, such as a legal licence rule or a published placement record.
    Both numbers are heuristics, not laws.
  </p>

  <p>
    Apply it to the fees above. A Rs 6 to 12 lakh nursing fee passes the 20% test only for a family with a
    budget of about Rs 30 to 60 lakh. A Rs 55 lakh management-quota seat needs a budget of about Rs 2.75 crore
    to pass the same test.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When paying more can make sense</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A licensed route where a recognised degree is legally required, such as nursing or physiotherapy.</li>
        <li>A college that publishes its batch size, placed count and median pay.</li>
        <li>Clinical or lab access that you cannot rebuild yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When it is likely waste</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A private college with no published placement record in a state with many unfilled seats.</li>
        <li>A fee that would need a loan and leaves nothing for tools or a skill course.</li>
        <li>Any seat sold as "no NEET" for a course that legally needs it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Many science skills can be built cheaply. Lab methods, data handling, science writing and basic bioinformatics
    can be practised with good YouTube channels, open university courses and low-cost online courses, then shown
    through a real project. A certificate is not proof; finished work is. Licensed routes such as nursing,
    pharmacy and physiotherapy still need the formal degree.
  </p>

